    "## 14. Edit a clip\nFixes one thing in a finished clip, such as an outfit colour or a prop, and keeps the rest of the take: the clip goes in as `<Video 1>` and the prompt asks for an edited version of it, the way MiniMax's guide writes a `video editing` task. The alternative is a re-render with a changed prompt, which keeps nothing but the seed.\n\n**Steps**\n1. Load the clip in **The clip to edit**: an H3 render at 24 fps. Set `length` on *Edit* to its frame count, since H3 cuts a reference to the render's length, and **set the noise seed to the seed the clip was rendered with** (the default is this clip's). On its own seed an edit kept 0.86 of the take (SSIM); on another seed, 0.57.\n2. Load the character's photos (the ones their RefCap was captured from). The clip carries the identity too; the photos hold it through the change.\n3. Write the prompt as the default does, from the clip's own prompt:\n   - `subject_definitions` starts with `<Video 1> is the source video for the target video edit.`\n   - `summary` is `[video editing + audio reuse] The target video is an edited version of <Video 1> in which ...; everything else stays as it is in <Video 1>.`\n   - `retention_analysis` has `<Video 1> (source video): partially_preserved - ... only the colour of her sweater differs.` and `<Audio 1>: fully_copy - ...`.\n   - `detailed_description` is the clip's own shot description with the change.\n\n**What to expect** (three H3 clips, edits of the sweater colour and an added apple; REFCAP_README.md, \"Video references\"):\n- Every edit applied its change: the sweater turned red 9 times out of 9 and the apple appeared 9 times out of 9, though sometimes as two or three apples.\n- An edit on the clip's own seed kept the take (SSIM 0.86). A re-render of the changed prompt on the same seed kept almost nothing (0.38-0.40).\n- The reused soundtrack is regenerated, not copied: voice similarity to the original was 0.83. A visual-only edit keeps the timing, so laying the original audio back on might do better (untested).\n\n**Cost:** the clip costs as many reference tokens as the render itself (9,823 at 608x352 and 158 frames, plus 80 per second of soundtrack): about 126 s per clip against 70 s for a re-render.\n\n**Settings in these workflows** follow ComfyUI's MiniMax H3 reference-to-video template: the ref2va model with its 4-step Lightning LoRA, 4 steps, `res_multistep` + `simple`, 608x352. For the base model, bypass the *Lightning LoRA* node and use 20 steps. The measurements quoted here ran on a community H3 turbo finetune (shift 6/3, 8 steps) on a 12 GB GPU. Raise the size and length for final renders; the reference tokens ride through every step, so more photos and larger captures cost time."
   ],
